/*
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * Build arm 4's devicetree half. Pass it WITH ti_coex.conf, never instead of
 * it:
 *
 *   west ... -- ... "-DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=ti_coex.conf" \
 *                   "-DEXTRA_DTC_OVERLAY_FILE=ti_coex.overlay"
 *
 * WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL, because "enable the 802.15.4 node" sounds like
 * something the driver's Kconfig would do and it is the other way round:
 * CONFIG_RADIANT_COEX154_CC26XX_FORK compiles the driver, but the driver
 * registers against DT_DRV_COMPAT ti,cc13xx-cc26xx-ieee802154, and Zephyr's
 * own board file (boards/ti/cc26x2r1_launchxl/cc26x2r1_launchxl.dts) both
 * disables that node and does `/delete-property/ zephyr,ieee802154`. Upstream,
 * not us - this LaunchXL ships with 802.15.4 off.
 *
 * The failure without this file is not a missing-driver message. It is
 *
 *   error: '__device_dts_ord_53' undeclared ... did you mean '__device_dts_ord_3'?
 *
 * from DEVICE_DT_INST_GET(0) inside the vendored driver: the node exists in the
 * tree but has no device, so the symbol the macro names was never generated.
 * That is a devicetree problem wearing a C compiler's clothes, and it is worth
 * recognising on sight because every other symptom in this arm looks like a
 * Kconfig one.
 *
 * `chosen` matters as much as `status`. Without zephyr,ieee802154 the driver
 * still builds and still registers, but NET_CONFIG's 802.15.4 setup has no
 * interface to configure, so the channel and PAN ID in ti_coex.conf are
 * silently applied to nothing and the neighbour sits on the default channel -
 * which is the one case this arm must not be in, since the channel is chosen
 * to keep the two stacks off each other's frequency (see ti_coex.conf).
 */

/ {
	chosen {
		zephyr,ieee802154 = &ieee802154;
	};
};

&ieee802154 {
	status = "okay";
};
